stairs-power-asides = The «horsepower» was thought up by the Scottish engineer James Watt in the late 18th century — to sell his steam engines to those who still turned their mills with horses. He reckoned: a mill horse walks a circle of 12-foot radius 144 times an hour and pulls with about 180 pounds-force — some 32,572 foot-pounds of work a minute. Watt and his partner Matthew Boulton rounded it to 33,000.
