stairs-power-build-question = We measure ourselves. Stand on the floor scales, measure a step with a ruler and count the steps to the next floor. Then walk up one floor at an easy pace, and then run up — a friend times you each time with a stopwatch. (Under the scene are your measurements: an example for now, put in your own.) What power do you develop running up the stairs?
stairs-power-answer-hp-of = hp
stairs-power-answer-why =
    Going up, you overcome your weight: your legs push you up with a force equal to your weight m·g over the floor’s height h. Mechanical work is a force times the distance along it: A = m·g·h. A step forward along the tread needs no work against your weight — only the height counts. So the stairs are the same — and so is the work, walking or running: your potential energy grows by just as much.

    But power is how fast the work is done: N = A/t. Running, you do the same work several times faster — and the power is that many times greater. For comparison: a healthy person can give about 70 W of mechanical power for a long time, and about 890 W in a short burst — ≈ 1.2 horsepower. So in a short burst a person can work even harder than a horse — but only for a few seconds. Compare with your own run.
